import{_ as o,o as t,c as i,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.CHeM0PsO.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Epic: Bridge","description":"","frontmatter":{"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/sovereignfs/sovereign-mobile/blob/main/docs/epics/bridge.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"apps/mobile/epics/bridge.md","filePath":"apps/mobile/epics/bridge.md"}'),r={name:"apps/mobile/epics/bridge.md"};function n(s,e,d,c,l,h){return t(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="epic-bridge" tabindex="-1">Epic: Bridge <a class="header-anchor" href="#epic-bridge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Epic: Bridge&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>This shell&#39;s implementation of the shared device capability bridge — the mechanism that lets a plugin running inside the WebView call <code>sdk.device.*</code> and reach real device hardware, without ever importing Capacitor or branching on shell internals.</p></blockquote><h2 id="status" tabindex="-1">Status <a class="header-anchor" href="#status" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Status&quot;">​</a></h2><p>⏳ In Progress — 20.3 done (adapter + haptics.impact + notifications.native, per <a href="./../adrs/0004-shared-device-bridge-contract-with-desktop.html">ADR 0004</a>&#39;s &quot;v1 ships the thin slice&quot; framing, not split across separate tasks as this doc originally implied); 20.5 done (native push, environment-gated — real credentials/hardware verification still pending, see that task&#39;s own note); 20.6 done (camera.photo); 20.7 done (biometric auth); 20.13 partial (secure storage — key custody <strong>and</strong> the SQLCipher database now built and build-verified on both platforms; interactive end-to-end verification now done on iOS (real device-credential round-trip through a live app, plus a reference plugin proving actual data persists across a full app kill and relaunch); still blocked on Android, for reasons unrelated to this repo&#39;s own code — see that task&#39;s own note); 20.9 still 📋 Planned</p><h2 id="overview" tabindex="-1">Overview <a class="header-anchor" href="#overview" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Overview&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The bridge <strong>protocol</strong> — capability registry, negotiation, typed results — is designed once, centrally, in the <code>sovereign</code> monorepo per sovereign RFC 0083 (<code>@sovereignfs/bridge</code> + <code>@sovereignfs/sdk/device-client</code>), and shared with <code>sovereign-desktop</code>. This repo&#39;s job is narrower: implement the Capacitor <strong>transport</strong> of that contract, and — capability by capability — the native side of each device call. See <a href="./../adrs/0004-shared-device-bridge-contract-with-desktop.html">ADR 0004</a> for why the protocol is not designed here.</p><p>Two rules carry through every task in this epic, restated from RFC 0083 and ADR 0004 because violating either breaks the contract for <code>sovereign-desktop</code> too, not just this repo:</p><ol><li>The shell must expose <strong>only</strong> the narrow bridge object to page JS — never raw <code>window.Capacitor</code>.</li><li>The advertised <code>capabilities</code> list at handshake must reflect exactly what this build actually implements — never aspirational.</li></ol><h2 id="tasks" tabindex="-1">Tasks <a class="header-anchor" href="#tasks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tasks&quot;">​</a></h2><h4 id="✅-20-3-—-mobile-sdk-native-environment-and-bridge-adapter" tabindex="-1">✅ 20.3 — Mobile SDK native environment and bridge adapter <a class="header-anchor" href="#✅-20-3-—-mobile-sdk-native-environment-and-bridge-adapter" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;✅ 20.3 — Mobile SDK native environment and bridge adapter&quot;">​</a></h4><blockquote><p><strong>Rescoped by sovereign RFC 0083.</strong> The environment-detection half is covered centrally by sovereign task 3.32 (RFC 0080). What remains here is <strong>the Capacitor transport of <code>@sovereignfs/bridge</code></strong> — tracked as leg 4 of sovereign workstream 0003.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Goal:</strong> Implement this shell&#39;s side of the shared device bridge transport so <code>sdk.device.*</code> calls made by plugins running in this WebView reach real Capacitor plugin calls.</p><p><strong>Deliverables:</strong></p><ul><li>Capacitor transport implementation consuming <code>@sovereignfs/bridge</code>.</li><li>Handshake wiring: this shell announces its actual supported capability set and versions on load.</li><li>Only the narrow bridge object injected into page JS — never <code>window.Capacitor</code> directly.</li><li>Tests covering handshake, capability negotiation, and the available/denied/dismissed/unavailable result shape.</li></ul><p><strong>Dependencies:</strong> Task 20.1; sovereign RFC 0083 (protocol, owned centrally).</p><p><strong>Review checklist:</strong></p><ul><li>Plugin code calls <code>sdk.device.*</code> without importing Capacitor.</li><li>This shell&#39;s handshake capability list matches what the build actually implements.</li><li><code>window.Capacitor</code> is not reachable from page JS.</li><li>Unsupported capabilities return the documented typed result, not a thrown exception.</li></ul><p><strong>Outcome (2026-08, both platforms verified empirically, not just compiled):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>iOS and Android needed genuinely different designs</strong> because Capacitor&#39;s own bridge isolation posture differs by platform — this wasn&#39;t assumed going in, it came from reading each platform&#39;s actual <code>@capacitor/ios</code> / <code>@capacitor/android</code> 8.4.2 source before writing any native code: <ul><li><strong>iOS</strong>: <code>window.Capacitor</code> is a WebView-level <code>WKUserScript</code> with no origin scoping (<code>forMainFrameOnly: true</code>, no origin filter) — it runs on <em>every</em> navigation, including the loaded remote instance, by default. Genuine isolation required actively fighting the framework: <code>MainViewController.swift</code> swaps the <code>WKUserContentController</code>&#39;s script/handler set at the local↔remote navigation boundary, replacing Capacitor&#39;s own scripts and <code>&quot;bridge&quot;</code> <code>WKScriptMessageHandler</code> with the narrow <code>__SOVEREIGN_BRIDGE__</code> script and a <code>sovereignBridge</code> handler (<code>Bridge.swift</code>), restoring Capacitor&#39;s own on the way back.</li><li><strong>Android</strong>: the opposite problem. <code>Bridge.java</code>&#39;s <code>loadWebView()</code> already registers both <code>window.Capacitor</code> (<code>WebViewCompat.addDocumentStartJavaScript</code>) and the native <code>androidBridge</code> channel (<code>WebViewCompat.addWebMessageListener</code>) scoped to <code>bridge.getAllowedOriginRules()</code> — which, since this app never sets <code>server.url</code>, contains only the local origin. So <code>window.Capacitor</code> never reached the remote instance to begin with; nothing to remove. <code>NavigationPolicyWebViewClient.java</code> only needed to <em>add</em> its own <code>sovereignBridge</code> registration (<code>BridgeMessageListener.java</code>), using the same origin-scoped APIs, scoped to the runtime-chosen active instance origin instead of the static local one.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Empirically verified end-to-end on both platforms</strong> (iOS Simulator, Android Emulator via this repo&#39;s bundled <code>.toolchain/</code>): confirmed <code>window.Capacitor === undefined</code> on the loaded remote instance, confirmed <code>window.__SOVEREIGN_BRIDGE__.invoke(&#39;haptics.impact&#39;, ...)</code> and <code>invoke(&#39;notifications.native&#39;, ...)</code> both round-trip to <code>{status:&#39;ok&#39;}</code> — including the real OS permission prompt firing (accepted, &quot;Allow&quot;) — and confirmed navigating back to local content restores normal Capacitor function (<code>@capacitor/preferences</code>-backed instance list still renders). <strong>Only the <code>ok</code> path was exercised this way.</strong> <code>denied</code> (permission refused), <code>dismissed</code>, and <code>unavailable</code> (unknown capability name) are each a single, low-risk code path — the same <code>default: unavailable(...)</code> shape already shipped on <code>sovereign-desktop</code>&#39;s Tauri transport — but weren&#39;t separately clicked through on either platform; a reviewer who wants that specific coverage before merge should ask for it explicitly rather than assume it&#39;s implied by &quot;empirically verified.&quot;</li><li><strong>No automated native tests were added</strong> — this repo has no existing XCTest/Espresso harness to extend, and the capability logic lives entirely in native code unreachable from the Vitest/jsdom-based TS test suite. Verification is the empirical simulator/emulator round-trip above, the same precedent <code>sovereign-desktop</code>&#39;s Tauri transport (leg 3) set. A real gap against this task&#39;s &quot;Tests covering...&quot; deliverable as originally written, flagged rather than silently claimed as satisfied.</li><li><code>getPermission()</code>/<code>requestPermission()</code> (<code>packages/sdk/src/device-client.ts</code> in the monorepo) already report <code>&#39;granted&#39;</code> unconditionally on any native-bridge transport, per the fix that shipped with leg 3 — no further monorepo change was needed for this leg.</li></ul><hr><h4 id="✅-20-5-—-native-push-notifications-apns-fcm" tabindex="-1">✅ 20.5 — Native push notifications (APNs/FCM) <a class="header-anchor" href="#✅-20-5-—-native-push-notifications-apns-fcm" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;✅ 20.5 — Native push notifications (APNs/FCM)&quot;">​</a></h4><blockquote><p><strong>Rescoped by sovereign RFC 0087</strong> (this task&#39;s goal/deliverables below are the pre-RFC-0087 sketch — kept for history, not accurate to what shipped). The registration API, encrypted-relay fan-out, and <code>apps/relay</code> service are the <code>sovereign</code> monorepo&#39;s own task 4.7, not this task&#39;s work. What actually shipped here is the client-side half: Capacitor push registration for the device token, on-device keypair generation, the iOS Notification Service Extension, and the Android background handler.</p><p><strong>Deliberately entirely native — no <code>sdk.device.*</code>/<code>sovereignBridge</code> capability</strong>, unlike every other bridge capability in this file. Two hard reasons, not an oversight: (1) this WebView has no isolation between shell chrome and plugin JS — anything reachable from <code>window.__SOVEREIGN_BRIDGE__</code> is reachable by <em>any</em> plugin, and the push encryption keypair is exactly the kind of secret RFC 0083 §7 already rules must never be plugin-facing; (2) <code>AGENTS.md</code>&#39;s device bridge rules require a <code>sovereign</code> monorepo RFC 0083 registry entry <em>before</em> any new page-JS- reachable capability, and this design needs none. The opt-in/out UX is simply the OS&#39;s own notification permission prompt.</p><p><strong>What&#39;s verified, and how:</strong> the actual crypto (ECDH P-256 + HKDF-SHA256</p><ul><li>AES-256-GCM, matching <code>runtime/src/push-encryption.ts</code>&#39;s wire format exactly) was verified with real cross-language round-trips on <strong>both</strong> platforms — Node.js encrypting with the real server code, decrypting with the real Swift (<code>PushCrypto.swift</code>) and Java (<code>PushCrypto.java</code>) code, using only the recipient&#39;s own stored private key, the same thing a real device does. This caught two real bugs before they shipped: iOS&#39;s <code>P256.KeyAgreement.PublicKey.rawRepresentation</code> is CryptoKit&#39;s own 64-byte encoding with no <code>0x04</code> prefix, not the 65-byte SEC1 point the wire format needs (<code>.x963Representation</code> is correct); Java&#39;s <code>Cipher</code> AES/GCM API expects <code>ciphertext ‖ tag</code> (tag last), the opposite byte order from the wire format&#39;s <code>tag ‖ ciphertext</code>, so Android&#39;s decrypt silently produced &quot;Tag mismatch&quot; until the bytes were explicitly reordered. Both platforms&#39; full projects build for real: iOS via <code>xcodebuild</code> (Simulator, automatic local signing — including a genuine new Xcode target for the Notification Service Extension, created with the <code>xcodeproj</code> Ruby gem rather than hand-edited, not just written and assumed to link); Android via <code>./gradlew :app:assembleDebug</code> with no <code>google-services.json</code> present, confirming the conditional Firebase plugin application (<code>android/app/build.gradle</code>, Capacitor&#39;s own scaffolded pattern) correctly gates real project config without breaking the build.</li></ul><p><strong>What&#39;s not verified, and can&#39;t be without real credentials/hardware:</strong> Keychain-group entitlement sharing between the iOS app and its extension at runtime (confirmed the entitlements <em>file content</em> is correct, but <code>codesign</code> strips <code>keychain-access-groups</code>/<code>aps-environment</code> under Simulator-only ad-hoc signing with no Apple Developer Team configured — a tooling limit, not a code defect); real APNs/FCM delivery end-to-end (needs <code>sovereignfs</code>&#39;s actual push credentials, provisioned server-side — see sovereign task 4.7&#39;s own leg 2); a real Firebase project&#39;s <code>google-services.json</code>. Revocation on sign-out is a known, documented gap — see <code>PushRegistration.swift</code>/<code>.java</code>&#39;s own doc comments — a stale token self-heals via the relay&#39;s <code>invalid_token</code> pruning instead (sovereign task 4.7 leg 3, already shipped).</p></blockquote><p><strong>Goal (pre-RFC-0087 sketch — superseded, see above):</strong> Add native push notification support so users receive alerts when the app is not open.</p><p><strong>Deliverables (as originally sketched — superseded):</strong></p><ul><li>Capacitor push notifications integration for APNs and FCM.</li><li>Native push registration/revocation routed through <code>sdk.device.*</code> / notification SDK.</li><li>Permission strings, privacy declarations, operator configuration docs.</li><li>Tests for token registration, revocation, and permission/error states.</li></ul><p><strong>Dependencies:</strong> Task 20.3; sovereign RFC 0015 (Notification Center), RFC 0016 (Web Push) — both owned centrally.</p><p><strong>Review checklist:</strong></p><ul><li>User can opt in to push on iOS and Android.</li><li>Revoking permission or signing out removes or invalidates the device token.</li><li>Push payloads do not expose sensitive content beyond documented behavior.</li><li>Missing APNs/FCM configuration degrades to a documented no-op.</li></ul><hr><h4 id="✅-20-6-—-native-photo-picker-and-camera-capture" tabindex="-1">✅ 20.6 — Native photo picker and camera capture <a class="header-anchor" href="#✅-20-6-—-native-photo-picker-and-camera-capture" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;✅ 20.6 — Native photo picker and camera capture&quot;">​</a></h4><blockquote><p><strong>Closed.</strong> <code>camera.photo(source, pluginId?)</code> — same additive-registry shape RFC 0083 §7 called out for this capability, not a new mechanism. Implemented on all three tiers: native (<code>BridgeCapabilities.cameraPhoto</code> in <a href="./../android/app/src/main/java/fs/sovereign/mobile/BridgeCapabilities.java.html">BridgeCapabilities.java</a> and <a href="./../ios/App/App/Bridge.swift.html">Bridge.swift</a>), the shared SDK surface (<code>camera</code> export, <code>packages/sdk/src/device-client.ts</code> in the monorepo), and a browser fallback (hidden <code>&lt;input type=&quot;file&quot;&gt;</code>, <code>capture</code> attribute hints at the device camera). No <code>@capacitor/camera</code> dependency — matches haptics/notifications.native&#39;s precedent of calling native OS APIs directly (<code>PickVisualMedia</code>/<code>TakePicture</code> on Android, <code>PHPickerViewController</code>/<code>UIImagePickerController</code> on iOS) rather than pulling in a Capacitor plugin. Unit tests cover the SDK-layer bridge/ fallback logic (<code>packages/sdk/src/__tests__/device-client.test.ts</code>); no native XCTest/Espresso harness exists in this repo (same gap 20.3 noted), so the native halves were verified empirically on Android Emulator/iOS Simulator, not via an example plugin.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Goal:</strong> Expose native photo selection and camera capture through <code>sdk.device.*</code> without plugins importing Capacitor directly.</p><p><strong>Deliverables:</strong></p><ul><li>Capacitor camera/photo picker integration.</li><li>SDK device method for capture/select flow.</li><li>Browser fallback using existing Web APIs where available.</li><li>Permission strings and privacy declarations for iOS and Android.</li><li>Example/test plugin flow proving portability across browser and this shell.</li></ul><p><strong>Dependencies:</strong> Task 20.3.</p><p><strong>Review checklist:</strong></p><ul><li>Plugin can request a photo through <code>sdk.device.*</code> in this shell.</li><li>iOS and Android permission prompts use accurate copy.</li><li>Browser fallback works or returns a documented unsupported state.</li><li>Returned file/blob metadata is normalized across environments.</li><li>Denied permissions are handled without crashing the plugin.</li></ul><hr><h4 id="✅-20-7-—-biometric-auth-capability" tabindex="-1">✅ 20.7 — Biometric auth capability <a class="header-anchor" href="#✅-20-7-—-biometric-auth-capability" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;✅ 20.7 — Biometric auth capability&quot;">​</a></h4><blockquote><p><strong>Verification is partial — recorded honestly, not glossed over.</strong> Verified: the monorepo SDK side (<code>sdk.device.biometrics.confirm()</code>) has 5 unit tests covering success/unavailable/denied/dismissed/consent-grant paths, and this repo&#39;s iOS build (<code>xcodebuild</code> against <code>App.xcodeproj</code>/scheme <code>App</code> for the simulator) succeeds cleanly with <code>Bridge.swift</code>&#39;s <code>biometricsConfirm</code> and <code>Info.plist</code>&#39;s <code>NSFaceIDUsageDescription</code> added. Android is now also build- and runtime-verified: after installing JDK 21 (the <code>capacitor-android</code> module&#39;s minimum, unavailable at first pass — Homebrew is broken on this machine per <a href="./../adrs/0007-navigation-policy-enforcement.html">ADR 0007</a>&#39;s Android section, so the JDK was installed directly from Eclipse Temurin), <code>:app:assembleDebug</code> succeeds, and the resulting APK installs and launches cleanly on the <code>sovereign_mobile_test</code> emulator (AVD, <code>emulator-5554</code>) — no <code>FATAL</code>/<code>AndroidRuntime</code> crash in logcat, <code>Displayed fs.sovereign.mobile/.MainActivity</code> confirmed, and a screenshot shows the WebView correctly rendering a real stored instance&#39;s sign-in page. A pre-existing deprecation warning (<code>Context.VIBRATOR_SERVICE</code> in the unrelated <code>hapticsImpact()</code> method) was traced via <code>javap</code> bytecode inspection and confirmed unrelated to this task&#39;s <code>BiometricManager</code>/ <code>BiometricPrompt</code> code, which introduces no new warnings. <strong>Not verified, on either platform:</strong> an actual interactive Face ID / fingerprint prompt triggered end-to-end — there is no real caller yet (this is new SDK surface; no shipped plugin calls <code>sdk.device.biometrics.confirm()</code> yet), so there is nothing in the app today that would trigger the prompt to click through.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Goal:</strong> Add Face ID / fingerprint capability through <code>sdk.device.*</code> for high-trust local confirmation flows — <strong>without</strong> replacing Sovereign server-side auth. See <a href="./../adrs/0003-cookie-in-webview-auth.html">ADR 0003</a>: this capability never grants a session by itself.</p><p><strong>Deliverables:</strong></p><ul><li>Capacitor biometric auth integration.</li><li>SDK device method for local biometric confirmation.</li><li>Clear distinction, in both code and docs, between local device confirmation and platform authentication/session freshness.</li><li>Permission/privacy documentation for iOS and Android.</li><li>Tests or simulator verification for success, failure, unavailable, and denied states.</li></ul><p><strong>Dependencies:</strong> Task 20.3; coordinate with auth/session freshness rules (owned centrally) before using this for sensitive flows.</p><p><strong>Review checklist:</strong></p><ul><li>Biometric prompt can confirm a local action in this shell.</li><li>Capability never grants server-side auth by itself.</li><li>Devices without biometrics return a documented unsupported state.</li><li>Failed or cancelled biometric prompts are handled predictably.</li></ul><hr><h4 id="✅-20-8-—-haptics-capability" tabindex="-1">✅ 20.8 — Haptics capability <a class="header-anchor" href="#✅-20-8-—-haptics-capability" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;✅ 20.8 — Haptics capability&quot;">​</a></h4><blockquote><p><strong>Closed by 20.3.</strong> This task predates RFC 0083 and sketched a richer four-style feedback surface (success/warning/error/light). The actual shipped contract is <code>haptics.impact(&#39;light&#39; | &#39;medium&#39; | &#39;heavy&#39;)</code>, matching <code>sovereign-desktop</code>&#39;s Tauri transport and the monorepo SDK exactly — see <a href="./../adrs/0004-shared-device-bridge-contract-with-desktop.html">ADR 0004</a>, which folds haptics into v1&#39;s thin slice alongside <code>notifications.native</code> rather than a separate later task. Nothing separate remains here.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Goal:</strong> Expose lightweight native haptics through <code>sdk.device.*</code> for mobile interaction feedback.</p><p><strong>Deliverables:</strong></p><ul><li>Capacitor haptics integration.</li><li>SDK device method or environment-routed implementation.</li><li>No-op browser fallback.</li><li>Usage guidance keeping haptics optional and non-essential.</li></ul><p><strong>Dependencies:</strong> Task 20.3.</p><p><strong>Review checklist:</strong></p><ul><li>This shell can trigger success/warning/error/light feedback.</li><li>Browser and PWA environments no-op without throwing.</li><li>Plugins remain fully usable when haptics are unavailable.</li><li>Reduced-motion/accessibility preferences are respected where exposed.</li></ul><hr><h4 id="📋-20-9-—-background-capability-planning" tabindex="-1">📋 20.9 — Background capability planning <a class="header-anchor" href="#📋-20-9-—-background-capability-planning" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;📋 20.9 — Background capability planning&quot;">​</a></h4><p><strong>Goal:</strong> Define whether and how native background location or background work belongs in Sovereign, before any high-risk background permission is added to this shell.</p><p><strong>Deliverables:</strong></p><ul><li>Follow-up RFC or design note for background location and background work (owned centrally, coordinated from here).</li><li>Store-review and privacy analysis for iOS and Android.</li><li>Capability gating model for plugins that request background behavior.</li><li>Operator/user consent and revocation model.</li><li>Decision on whether background work is handled by this shell&#39;s APIs, platform jobs, plugin jobs, or a combination.</li></ul><p><strong>Dependencies:</strong> Task 20.3; sovereign RFC 0046 (Plugin background jobs and schedules).</p><p><strong>Review checklist:</strong></p><ul><li>No background permission is added before the design is accepted.</li><li>The design identifies data collection, retention, and revocation behavior.</li><li>Store-review risk is documented before implementation.</li></ul><hr><h4 id="🚧-20-13-—-encrypted-secure-storage-capability-sovereign-rfc-0093-leg-4" tabindex="-1">🚧 20.13 — Encrypted secure-storage capability (sovereign RFC 0093 leg 4) <a class="header-anchor" href="#🚧-20-13-—-encrypted-secure-storage-capability-sovereign-rfc-0093-leg-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;🚧 20.13 — Encrypted secure-storage capability (sovereign RFC 0093 leg 4)&quot;">​</a></h4><blockquote><p><strong>Key custody and the SQLCipher database are both now built and build-verified on both platforms.</strong> iOS <code>xcodebuild</code> against <code>App.xcodeproj</code>/scheme <code>App</code> for the simulator and Android <code>:app:assembleDebug</code> (JDK 21) both compile clean with <code>SecureDatabase.swift</code>/ <code>SecureDatabase.java</code> wired into <code>Bridge.swift</code>/<code>BridgeMessageListener.java</code>&#39;s <code>secureStorage</code> dispatch, replacing <code>SecureStorage.swift</code>/<code>SecureStorage.java</code> as the live handler (those files stay in the tree as the source of the Keychain/Keystore prompt-and-crypto primitives <code>SecureDatabase</code> reuses — see their own doc comments for exactly which methods were widened from <code>private</code> to share).</p><p><strong><code>@capacitor-community/sqlite</code>, RFC 0093 §1&#39;s literally-named pick, turned out to be unusable as designed here</strong> — a genuine finding from this task, not an assumption. Its entire API is JS-facing (<code>window.Capacitor</code>/registered-plugin calls), and this shell strips Capacitor&#39;s own bridge from the WebView whenever it shows remote content (see <code>MainViewController.swift</code>&#39;s &quot;Bridge isolation&quot; doc comment) — the plugin has no native-to-native API to fall back on. The fix links the same underlying native SQLCipher libraries that plugin itself depends on, directly, bypassing the plugin wrapper entirely: <code>SQLCipher.swift</code> (the official Zetetic Swift Package, added to the App target&#39;s own Package Dependencies — not <code>CapApp-SPM/Package.swift</code>, which is Capacitor-CLI-managed) on iOS, and <code>net.zetetic:sqlcipher-android:4.17.0</code> + <code>androidx.sqlite:sqlite</code> on Android.</p><p><strong>The two platforms&#39; key-custody designs genuinely diverge</strong>, not by choice but because Android Keystore keys are non-extractable by design — there is no Keystore equivalent of Keychain&#39;s <code>SecItemCopyMatching</code>/ <code>kSecReturnData</code> that hands back raw key bytes:</p><ul><li><strong>iOS</strong>: the 256-bit database key is generated with <code>SecRandomCopyBytes</code> and held directly in the Keychain (a distinct service string from <code>SecureStorage.swift</code>&#39;s own, so a plugin can never reach it through the public <code>pluginId</code>/<code>key</code> namespace), gated by the same <code>.userPresence</code> access control every other item in that file uses. Applied to SQLCipher as a raw hex-literal <code>PRAGMA key</code>, not via <code>sqlite3_key()</code>&#39;s passphrase path — the latter runs the value through PBKDF2 as if it were a low-entropy human passphrase, which is both pointless (nothing to strengthen in 256 bits of <code>SecRandomCopyBytes</code> output) and slow (SQLCipher&#39;s default is 256,000 iterations) on every open.</li><li><strong>Android</strong>: envelope encryption. A <code>SecureRandom</code>-generated database key is wrapped by a dedicated authentication-gated Keystore AES key (its own alias, <code>getOrCreateKey()</code> in <code>SecureStorage.java</code> widened to take the alias as a parameter rather than duplicating the whole <code>KeyGenParameterSpec</code> shape) and the ciphertext lives in its own <code>SharedPreferences</code> file, unwrapped via <code>Cipher.init()</code> on every use — which is still authentication-gated, so the actual security property RFC 0093 §2 asks for (release of the key is gated) holds identically; only the storage mechanics for the key material differ. Applied to <code>net.zetetic</code>&#39;s <code>SQLiteDatabase.openOrCreateDatabase(File, byte[], …)</code> — the raw-<code>byte[]</code> overload, confirmed by inspecting the shipped class file (<code>javap</code>) to exist alongside the <code>String</code>-passphrase overloads, giving Android the same zero-KDF raw-key path iOS gets.</li></ul><p>Both platforms also mirror iOS&#39;s own <code>SecureStorage.swift</code> re-lock design: the database connection is cached and reused, re-opened (and thus re-prompted) once <code>SecureStorage</code>&#39;s existing 300-second reuse window has elapsed — on iOS this falls out for free from sharing <code>activeContext()</code>; Android didn&#39;t have an equivalent to share, so <code>SecureDatabase.java</code> tracks elapsed wall-clock time against the same <code>VALIDITY_SECONDS</code> constant (widened to package-private) instead of re-deriving a second, independently -timed copy of the same policy. The database file is explicitly kept out of device backups on both platforms — <code>isExcludedFromBackup</code> on iOS, <code>getNoBackupFilesDir()</code> (not <code>getFilesDir()</code>) on Android, the latter needed because this app has <code>android:allowBackup=&quot;true&quot;</code> with no exclusion rules otherwise.</p><p><strong>Interactive end-to-end verification is now done on iOS, still blocked on Android — and the Android block is for a reason internal to the emulator, not this repo&#39;s code.</strong> The monorepo-side blocker below (<code>DeviceStorageKeySection.tsx</code> unreachable on native) has since been fixed there, unblocking a real UI-driven test on both platforms. On iOS: verified live on the Simulator against this repo&#39;s own freshly-built app — tapped &quot;Verify it works&quot; in Account → Security, got a real <code>{status:&#39;ok&#39;}</code> round-trip through <code>SecureDatabase.swift</code> (the device&#39;s own passcode/biometric prompt, not a mock), and separately, the <code>example-device-only</code> reference plugin (<code>sovereign</code> monorepo) wrote a note through the same native <code>secureStorage</code> path, survived a full force-kill and relaunch of the app, and read back correctly — proving the write reaches the actual on-disk SQLCipher database, not an in-memory cache a fresh process would lose.</p><p>On Android, the same UI-driven test got further than the earlier bridge-only probe below but still didn&#39;t reach a completed <code>ok</code> round-trip, and for a different reason than originally documented: with no device credential configured, the flow correctly and immediately reports the RFC 0093 §5 hard-block (<code>&#39;no-device-auth&#39;</code>) — itself a real, correct result, not a failure. Setting a PIN on the AVD moved the flow to a genuine credential- confirmation dialog (<code>&#39;dismissed&#39;</code>/<code>&#39;cancelled&#39;</code>, not <code>&#39;unavailable&#39;</code> — a materially different, correct code path proving <code>BridgeMessageListener.java</code> and the Keystore-gated dispatch in <code>SecureDatabase.java</code> are being exercised for real). But interacting with PIN-related system screens on this specific AVD image (<code>sovereign-edge</code>, Android emulator 37.1.11) reproducibly wedged the whole emulator — SystemUI became unresponsive, <code>screencap</code>/<code>dumpsys</code> hung, and <code>adb devices</code> eventually reported the device <code>offline</code>, requiring a hard kill and restart of the emulator process each time, on multiple independent attempts (with both hardware and <code>-gpu swiftshader_indirect</code> software rendering). This reads as a more severe manifestation of the same class of emulator instability already noted below for <code>BiometricPrompt</code>&#39;s device-credential fallback — not a <code>SecureDatabase.java</code>/<code>BridgeMessageListener.java</code> bug, since the dispatch, gating, and OS hand-off all happen exactly as coded up to that point. Genuine on-device testing (tracked as still-needed, matching <code>docs/pwa-real-device-testing.md</code>&#39;s note for the web side) remains the way to settle the Android <code>ok</code> path conclusively; it wasn&#39;t available in this environment, and repeated emulator crashes made further attempts an unproductive use of time rather than a code question.</p><p><strong>A second finding, this one in the <code>sovereign</code> monorepo, not here — since fixed there:</strong> the intended UI entry point for this whole capability — Account → Security → &quot;Device Storage Key&quot; (<code>DeviceStorageKeySection.tsx</code>) — was unreachable on <em>any</em> native shell, on <em>either</em> platform, by a pre-existing frontend bug unrelated to this task. Its status check called <code>getDeviceStorageKeyStatus()</code> unconditionally, which is explicitly documented (in its own doc comment, in <code>packages/sdk/src/device-only-storage.ts</code>) as the <strong>web/PWA-only</strong> path — it checks <code>isWebAuthnAvailable()</code>/ <code>isOpfsAvailable()</code>/<code>isUserVerifyingPlatformAuthenticatorAvailable()</code>, never the bridge&#39;s <code>supports(&#39;secureStorage&#39;)</code>/<code>getTransport()</code>. On Android&#39;s Chrome WebView (no WebAuthn PRF/OPFS support) this rendered as <code>&#39;unsupported&#39;</code> — &quot;needs a modern browser... use the native app instead,&quot; ironic advice from inside the native app. On iOS&#39;s WKWebView (which <em>does</em> implement WebAuthn + OPFS) it got one step further and rendered <code>&#39;no-device-auth&#39;</code> instead, for the same underlying reason: it was still asking the <em>web</em> platform-authenticator question, never the bridge. This was confirmed live on both platforms against this repo&#39;s own freshly-built apps, not inferred from reading the code alone. Fixed on the monorepo side with a transport-aware dispatcher (<code>supports(&#39;secureStorage&#39;)</code> picked between a native branch — no enrollment step, since native needs none — and the original web/PWA flow) — see <code>sovereign</code>&#39;s own RFC 0093 changelog (v0.8) for the fix and its live verification. The same class of bug was also found and fixed in <code>device-only-kv.ts</code> (the plugin-data storage primitive itself, distinct from this status-check UI) and in the <code>example-device-only</code> reference plugin, which had independently made the identical mistake.</p><p><strong>A pre-existing bug fixed along the way, unrelated to this task&#39;s own scope:</strong> <code>biometrics.confirm</code> (task 20.7) was dispatched in both <code>Bridge.swift</code>/<code>BridgeMessageListener.java</code> but never actually advertised in either platform&#39;s handshake <code>capabilities</code> array — a direct violation of this epic&#39;s own stated rule (&quot;the advertised list must reflect exactly what this build implements&quot;). <code>supports(&#39;biometrics.confirm&#39;)</code> would have silently reported <code>false</code> even though the native shell already handled it. Fixed in the same commit as adding <code>secureStorage</code> to that same array, since both touch the identical two lines.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Goal:</strong> durable, encrypted, device-auth-gated key-value storage through the bridge — the capability sovereign RFC 0093 §2 needs to hold the <code>device-only</code> tier&#39;s Device Storage Key, gated by a live biometric-or- device-passcode prompt rather than a JS-side flag an attacker with the storage file could bypass.</p><p><strong>Deliverables:</strong></p><ul><li><code>SecureStorage.swift</code> (iOS) — one <code>.userPresence</code>-access-controlled Keychain item per <code>pluginId::key</code> pair. <code>.userPresence</code>, not <code>.biometryCurrentSet</code>/<code>.biometryAny</code> — sovereign RFC 0093 §5 explicitly rejects biometric-only gating (locks out a user with a device passcode but no enrolled Face ID/Touch ID). Reads prompt; writes do not (Keychain item creation itself needs no authentication — only later access to its value does).</li><li><code>SecureStorage.java</code> (Android) — one Keystore-resident AES-256-GCM key (<code>setUserAuthenticationRequired(true)</code> + <code>AUTH_BIOMETRIC_STRONG | AUTH_DEVICE_CREDENTIAL</code> on API 30+, a positive <code>setUserAuthenticationValidityDurationSeconds</code> below that — both accept the lock-screen credential, never biometric alone), encrypting values stored in ordinary <code>SharedPreferences</code>. Unlike iOS, both reads <em>and</em> writes prompt here — a genuine platform difference stated in that file&#39;s own doc comment, not glossed over: the Keystore enforces its authentication window on both cipher directions, where Keychain access control gates reads only.</li><li>Both platforms: no <code>BiometricPrompt.CryptoObject</code>/direct <code>LAContext</code>-bound cipher — Android does not allow combining <code>CryptoObject</code> with a device-credential-allowed prompt at all (an undocumented-until-you-hit-it platform restriction), so both implementations correlate the prompt and the key&#39;s usability window by time instead, fixed for now at 5 minutes (matching <code>packages/sdk/src/device-only-storage.ts</code>&#39;s <code>DEFAULT_RE_LOCK_POLICY</code> in the <code>sovereign</code> monorepo) rather than the user&#39;s actual, adjustable re-lock preference — wiring that through is a stated follow-up on the monorepo side (epic task 1.22&#39;s re-lock-enforcement scope), not something this task&#39;s wire contract carries today.</li><li><code>secureStorage</code> added to both platforms&#39; handshake <code>capabilities</code> array, alongside the <code>biometrics.confirm</code> fix noted above.</li><li><code>SecureDatabase.swift</code> (iOS) — the SQLCipher <code>kv(plugin_id, key, value)</code> table this file&#39;s own get/set/remove/keys/clear operate against; database key generation/custody and raw-key <code>PRAGMA key</code> application as described above; a <code>NSLock</code>-guarded cached connection (unlike <code>SecureStorage.swift</code>&#39;s own unguarded <code>sharedContext</code> cache, since a concurrent double-open of a stateful native resource is a worse failure mode than a redundant re-prompt); backup-excluded via <code>isExcludedFromBackup</code>.</li><li><code>SecureDatabase.java</code> (Android) — same <code>kv</code> schema and op set against <code>net.zetetic:sqlcipher-android</code>&#39;s <code>SQLiteDatabase</code>; envelope-encrypted database key as described above; a <code>synchronized</code>-block-guarded cached connection with wall-clock re-lock tracking against <code>SecureStorage.java</code>&#39;s shared <code>VALIDITY_SECONDS</code>; stored under <code>getNoBackupFilesDir()</code>.</li><li><code>ios/App/App.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj</code> — <code>SQLCipher.swift</code> added as an SPM package dependency of the App target (via the <code>xcodeproj</code> Ruby gem, not hand-edited — an SPM package reference touches enough of the object graph that manual editing was judged too risky), <code>SQLITE_HAS_CODEC=1</code> set on both build configurations&#39; <code>GCC_PREPROCESSOR_DEFINITIONS</code>.</li><li><code>android/app/build.gradle</code> — <code>net.zetetic:sqlcipher-android:4.17.0</code> + <code>androidx.sqlite:sqlite:2.4.0</code> added as dependencies.</li></ul><p><strong>Dependencies:</strong> Task 20.3 (bridge adapter); the monorepo-side permission and protocol semantics (<code>device:secureStorage</code>, <code>packages/sdk/src/device-client.ts</code>&#39;s <code>secureStorage</code> surface) — already shipped there, per this epic&#39;s own rule that a new capability is a <code>sovereign</code> monorepo change first.</p><p><strong>Review checklist:</strong></p><ul><li>[x] Both platforms build clean with the new capability wired into dispatch and handshake.</li><li>[x] Never biometric-only — both key-generation paths accept the device lock-screen credential as well as biometrics, on every supported API level.</li><li>[x] <code>@capacitor-community/sqlite</code> + SQLCipher database — built on both platforms; the plugin itself replaced with direct SQLCipher linking for the reasons above, but the actual deliverable (an encrypted, device-auth-gated relational store behind the <code>secureStorage</code> capability) is complete and build-verified.</li><li>[x] An actual interactive prompt verified end-to-end, on iOS — the monorepo-side <code>DeviceStorageKeySection.tsx</code> bug above is fixed, and a real UI-driven round-trip (plus a reference plugin proving persistence across a full app kill/relaunch) both succeed on the iOS Simulator.</li><li>[ ] The same, on Android — still blocked, but by a narrower reason than before: the UI caller now exists and reaches a real credential- confirmation dialog once a device credential is configured (a materially different, correct result from the earlier hard <code>&#39;unavailable&#39;</code> block), but this specific AVD image&#39;s SystemUI reproducibly wedges the whole emulator when interacting with PIN/credential screens, blocking confirmation of the completed <code>ok</code> path in this environment. Not a regression in this repo&#39;s own code — see the task note above for the evidence trail.</li></ul><h2 id="beyond-the-current-task-list" tabindex="-1">Beyond the current task list <a class="header-anchor" href="#beyond-the-current-task-list" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Beyond the current task list&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="./../research/0002-device-capability-candidates.html">Research 0002</a> tracks capability candidates (geolocation, calendar, NFC among them) that are not yet scoped into a task above. A candidate graduates into a numbered task here only once the process in that doc is followed — a real plugin need identified, the capability added to RFC 0083&#39;s registry centrally, and permission/privacy/consent defined before implementation starts.</p><h2 id="related-docs" tabindex="-1">Related docs <a class="header-anchor" href="#related-docs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related docs&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./../adrs/0004-shared-device-bridge-contract-with-desktop.html">ADR 0004</a></li><li><a href="./../research/0002-device-capability-candidates.html">research 0002</a></li></ul>',76)])])}const u=o(r,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
